Description: Implements quantile-on-quantile regression (QQR) as introduced
    by Sim and Zhou (2015) <doi:10.1016/j.jbankfin.2015.02.030> for
    exploring how the effect of a covariate varies jointly across the
    conditional quantiles of a response and the quantiles of a designated
    regressor. The main fitting function 'qqr()' uses local linear quantile
    regression with Gaussian kernel weights to estimate coefficient and
    p-value surfaces over user-specified grids of outcome and regressor
    quantiles. Eight automatic bandwidth selection methods are provided to
    promote reproducibility and remove researcher discretion over this
    critical tuning parameter: Silverman's (1986) rule of thumb, a robust
    IQR-corrected rule (Scott 1992), an IQR-scaled rule, a fast
    leave-one-out cross-validation selector (Yu and Jones 1998, 'cv_fast'),
    the Sheather-Jones solve-the-equation plug-in
    (Sheather and Jones 1991, 'sj'), the Ruppert-Sheather-Wand direct
    plug-in for local linear regression (Ruppert, Sheather and Wand 1995,
    'yj'), np full least-squares cross-validation via 'npregbw()'
    (Hayfield and Racine 2008, 'npcv'), and np smooth-coefficient
    cross-validation via 'npscoefbw()' (Li and Racine 2010, 'sc').
    The selected bandwidth, formula, numerical inputs and bibliographic
    reference are stored in the fitted object and printed automatically.
    A comprehensive visualisation suite covers every major display type
    for QQR surfaces: static and interactive three-dimensional perspective
    plots ('surface3d()', 'panel_surface()', 'plot.qqr()', 'Int.plot()');
    publication-ready two-dimensional heatmaps with optional significance
    star overlays and symmetric zero-centred colour scaling
    ('qqr_heatmap()'); filled contour plots with automatic zero-contour
    highlighting and significance masking ('qqr_contour()'); a unified
    interactive plotly hub supporting 3D surface, heatmap and contour
    display types in a single function ('qqr_plotly()'); and a quantile
    dependence heatmap ('qqr_cor_heatmap()') that characterises the
    bivariate dependence structure between the response and the quantile
    regressor across a joint quantile grid using six dependence measures:
    Spearman rank correlation (Spearman 1904), Kendall's tau-b
    (Kendall 1938), Somers' asymmetric D statistic (Somers 1962),
    Pearson linear correlation, Blomqvist's medial beta
    (Blomqvist 1950), and co-exceedance probability, a tail-dependence
    measure widely used in financial contagion research. The dependence
    heatmap supports both a subsample mode, which computes measures on
    the actual data values within each joint quantile cell, and a binary
    indicator mode that replicates the approach of Roudane (2026).
    Additional methods support extracting surfaces as tidy tables
    ('qqr_surface()', 'rowave.qqr()'), exporting results with significance
    stars to Excel and Word ('excel.qqr()', 'word.qqr()'), and comparing
    row-averaged QQR effects with standard quantile regression coefficients
    ('compare.qqr()', 'plot.compare.qqr()', 'excel.compare.qqr()',
    'word.compare.qqr()'). Core quantile regression algorithms use
    'quantreg' (Koenker 2005 <doi:10.1017/CBO9780511754098>). Static
    heatmap and contour functions require 'ggplot2'; the interactive
    'qqr_plotly()' requires 'plotly'. The 'sj' and 'yj' bandwidth
    selectors require only base R or the recommended 'KernSmooth' package;
    the 'npcv' and 'sc' selectors require the 'np' package.
